{"id":"h17t3","name":"Peak Concurrent Streams","lang":"sql","entry":null,"params":[],"limit_s":10,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>The table <b>sessions</b> has the columns <code>session_id</code>, <code>user_id</code>, <code>started_at</code> and <code>ended_at</code> (timestamps). A session is active from <code>started_at</code> (included) to <code>ended_at</code> (excluded): a session that ends exactly when another one starts does <b>not</b> overlap it. A session with <code>ended_at</code> = <code>NULL</code> is still running and never ends. Sessions can cross midnight.</p>\n<p>The number of concurrent sessions changes only when a session starts or ends. For each day on which at least one session <b>started</b>, we want the highest number of concurrent sessions reached at a moment when a session of that day started, and the first moment it was reached that day.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return the columns <code>day</code> (date), <code>peak</code>, <code>peak_at</code> (timestamp) and <code>sessions_started</code> (number of sessions that started that day), sorted by <code>day</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p>Table <code>sessions</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>session_id</th><th>user_id</th><th>started_at</th><th>ended_at</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2026-03-02 08:00:00</td><td>2026-03-02 09:00:00</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2026-03-02 08:30:00</td><td>2026-03-02 09:30:00</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>2026-03-02 09:00:00</td><td>2026-03-02 10:00:00</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>2026-03-02 09:00:00</td><td>null</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>5</td><td>2026-03-02 09:40:00</td><td>2026-03-02 09:50:00</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>2026-03-02 23:50:00</td><td>2026-03-03 00:40:00</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>2</td><td>2026-03-03 00:30:00</td><td>2026-03-03 09:00:00</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>3</td><td>2026-03-03 08:10:00</td><td>2026-03-03 08:20:00</td></tr></table></div><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>day</th><th>peak</th><th>peak_at</th><th>sessions_started</th></tr><tr><td>2026-03-02</td><td>3</td><td>2026-03-02 09:00:00</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>2026-03-03</td><td>3</td><td>2026-03-03 00:30:00</td><td>2</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 10 seconds (psql)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 1 GB<br><b>[input]</b> tables <code>sessions</code><br><b>[output]</b> the result of one query with exactly the columns <code>day</code>, <code>peak</code>, <code>peak_at</code>, <code>sessions_started</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"/* Write your PostgreSQL query here */\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["événements +1 / -1","somme cumulée","ordre des égalités","sessions sans fin","UNION ALL"],"minutes":25,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>query.sql</code>. Efface le commentaire de départ si tu veux.</li><li>Écris <b>une seule requête</b> qui commence par <code>SELECT</code> (ou <code>WITH</code>). Les tables <code>sessions</code> existent déjà, remplies avec les données du test : pas de <code>CREATE TABLE</code> ni d'<code>INSERT</code>.</li><li>Le moteur est PostgreSQL. Si tu écris plusieurs requêtes, seul le résultat de la dernière est noté.</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Le résultat de ta requête doit avoir <b>exactement 4 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>day</code>, <code>peak</code>, <code>peak_at</code>, <code>sessions_started</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Nomme chaque colonne calculée avec <code>AS</code>.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>ORDER BY</code> à la fin de la requête).</li><li>Valeurs : les dates s'affichent AAAA-MM-JJ et AAAA-MM-JJ HH:MM:SS pour une date avec heure.</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 8 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"tables":{"sessions":{"columns":[["session_id","INT"],["user_id","INT"],["started_at","TIMESTAMP"],["ended_at","TIMESTAMP"]],"rows":[[1,1,"2026-03-02 08:00:00","2026-03-02 09:00:00"],[2,2,"2026-03-02 08:30:00","2026-03-02 09:30:00"],[3,3,"2026-03-02 09:00:00","2026-03-02 10:00:00"],[4,4,"2026-03-02 09:00:00",null],[5,5,"2026-03-02 09:40:00","2026-03-02 09:50:00"],[6,1,"2026-03-02 23:50:00","2026-03-03 00:40:00"],[7,2,"2026-03-03 00:30:00","2026-03-03 09:00:00"],[8,3,"2026-03-03 08:10:00","2026-03-03 08:20:00"]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["day","peak","peak_at","sessions_started"],"rows":[["2026-03-02",3,"2026-03-02 09:00:00",6],["2026-03-03",3,"2026-03-03 00:30:00",2]]}},{"input":{"tables":{"sessions":{"columns":[["session_id","INT"],["user_id","INT"],["started_at","TIMESTAMP"],["ended_at","TIMESTAMP"]],"rows":[[1,1,"2026-03-02 08:00:00","2026-03-02 08:10:00"],[2,1,"2026-03-02 08:10:00","2026-03-02 08:20:00"],[3,1,"2026-03-02 08:20:00","2026-03-02 08:30:00"]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["day","peak","peak_at","sessions_started"],"rows":[["2026-03-02",1,"2026-03-02 08:00:00",3]]}}],"hidden_n":8,"hidden":"","solution":""}